Y%C3%BC-sh%C3%AAng\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Liang Y\u00fc-sh\u00eang\">Liang Y\u00fc-sh\u00eang</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367407\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367407\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3643705</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 1 \u2014 Liang T&#39;ung-shu</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">Tu Lien-ch\u00ea</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"505\" data-page-number=\"505\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/517\" data-page-index=\"517\" data-page-quality=\"4\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/517\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;517\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>LIANG</b> T'ung-shu \u6881\u540c\u66f8 (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u5143\u9834,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u5c71\u821f, \u4e0d\u7fc1, \u65b0\u543e\u9577\u7fc1), Oct. 26, 1723\u20131815, Aug. 19, scholar and calligrapher, was a native of Ch'ien-t'ang (Hangchow). He was the elder son of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Liang_Shih-ch%C3%AAng\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Liang Shih-ch\u00eang\">Liang Shih-ch\u00eang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] but was adopted by his uncle, Liang Ch'i-hsin \u6881\u555f\u5fc3 (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u9996\u5b58,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u850e\u6797,&#32;1695\u20131758, a <i>chin-shih</i> of 1739). In 1747 Liang Tsung-shu became a <i>ch\u00fc-j\u00ean</i>. Failing to pass the metropolitan examination of 1752, Emperor Kao-tsung granted him special permission to proceed to the palace examination, thus making it possible for him to take his <i>chin-shih</i> degree in that year and become a member of the Hanlin Academy. In 1756 he was appointed associate examiner of the Shun-t'ien provincial examination, and in the following year, of the metropolitan examination. After the death of his foster father in 1758, he abandoned his official career and retired to private life. Nevertheless he presented himself in the capital in 1770 for the celebration of the eightieth birthday of Empress Hsiao-sh\u00eang (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Hung-li\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Hung-li\">Hung-li</a></span>), mother of Emperor Kao-tsung, and again twenty years later, for the eightieth birthday of Emperor Kao-tsung himself. In 1807, the sixtieth anniversary of his graduation as a <i>ch\u00fc-j\u00ean</i>, he participated in the banquet known as Lu-ming yen \u9e7f\u9cf4\u5bb4 which was given to newly-selected <i>ch\u00fc-j\u00ean</i>, and to older men who had passed a cycle since receiving the degree.\n</p><p>As a calligrapher Liang T'ung-shu was one of the foremost of the Ch'ing period. It is said that he could write characters three feet square. Facsimile inscriptions of his calligraphy are very numerous. His fame as a calligrapher even reached Japan and the Loochoo islands. Two contemporaries, likewise surnamed Liang, were also known as great calligraphers\u2014Liang Hsien \u6881\u5dd8 (<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u805e\u5c71,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u677e\u9f4b,&#32;a <i>ch\u00fc-j\u00ean</i> of 1762) and <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Liang_Kuo-chih\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Liang Kuo-chih\">Liang Kuo-chih</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>]. The three were known together as the three Liangs (\u4e09\u6881).\n</p><p>The collected works of Liang Tung-shu, entitled \u983b\u7f85\u5eb5\u907a\u96c6 <i>P'ing-lo an i-chi</i>, in 16 <i>ch\u00fcan</i>, were first printed in 1817. The last <i>ch\u00fcan</i> of this work, entitled \u7b46\u53f2 <i>Pi-shih</i>, contains a brief account of the development of the Chinese writing brush, together with information about well-known brush-makers and the materials they used. Liang T'ung-shu had no son, but adopted <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Liang_Y%C3%BC-sh%C3%AAng\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Liang Y\u00fc-sh\u00eang\">Liang Y\u00fc-sh\u00eang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], the eldest son of his younger brother. He is said to have led a very simple and regular life, and that he exercised great economy in the management of family affairs.\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[1/508/3a; 3/126/35b; 29/5/12b; <i>Hangchow fu chih</i> (1922) 146/6b.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Tu Lien-ch\u00ea</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Hung-li"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Liang Kuo-chih"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Liang Shih-ch\u00eang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Liang T'ing-nan"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Liang Y\u00fc-sh\u00eang"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Tu Lien-ch\u00ea"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 1\n | onlysection = liang t'ung-shu\n | previous = Liang T'ing-nan\n | next     = Liang Y\u00fc-sh\u00eang\n | from     = 517\n | to       = 517\n | notes    =\n | section_author = Tu Lien-ch\u00ea\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Liang T'ung-shu}}"}}}